y skirt. Objects: a thick stapled answer booklet of white A4 pages with fine grey scribble lines (no readable writing). Shot, 8 seconds, vertical 9:16: close-up across the centre aisle between two desks. 0-1.5 s: GLASSES BOY confidently holds out his ENTIRE thick stapled answer booklet across the aisle with one hand; BRAIDS GIRL grabs it with both hands, eyes wide with delight. 1.5-3 s: rack focus up to his face: a proud, smug grin and a wink, pushing his glasses up his nose. 3-8 s: she hugs the booklet to her chest and quickly starts copying, while he leans back with his arms crossed, very pleased with himself. Opening frame: his hand holding the booklet out over the aisle. Final frame: him leaning back smugly, her copying. Camera: low close-up at desk-top height looking along the aisle, a quick smooth whip-pan following the booklet, then a slow push-in on his face. Shallow depth of field. Setting and light: the same bright exam hall: pale teal walls, light wood desks and floor; bright soft high-key light, clean saturated colours. Real-world scale: the A4 booklet is about as long as his forearm. Sound: a fast paper whoosh as the booklet crosses the aisle, a crisp page flap, a chair creak, quiet room tone. No music. No speech, no voices, no narration, no singing. Composition: the main action in the middle of the frame; keep the bottom quarter simple (captions go there) and nothing important in the top-right corner (logo). Avoid: any text, letters, numbers, labels, subtitles, captions, logos or watermarks drawn in the scene; dark, muddy or gloomy lighting; photorealism; extra fingers or warped faces; characters changing appearance; jittery, morphing or teleporting motion; sudden scene changes or cuts inside the clip.